    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change mirrors the established convention in this codebase almost exactly. `Owner` already carries a family of derived, non-persisted getters \u2014 `getLocality()`, `getContactPreference()` (which returns the raw strings `\"EMAIL\"`/`\"PHONE\"` for an OpenAPI enum), `getMembershipLevel()`, `getCheckDigit()`, `getIdentityKey()`, `getBulkSignupWarning()`. `getAgeBand()` is the same shape as `getContactPreference()`: a request-time derivation returning a String enum value with a doc comment and a null/absent case. So:\n\n- **Right unit:** The derivation lives on the entity next to the other derived getters and the `birthDate`/`registrationDate` fields it reads \u2014 exactly where a maintainer of this file would look. Field, accessors, and derived getter are grouped sensibly.\n- **No duplication:** Nothing else computes age bands; the enum values appear only here and in the OpenAPI schema, matching how `contactPreference` is already handled (no centralized constant, consistent with the house style).\n- **Not a new catch-all:** `Owner` is already a hub of derived getters by design; this adds one more consistent member rather than introducing a new concern.\n- **Returning `String` over an enum type** is consistent with the existing `getContactPreference()` precedent, so it's the idiomatic choice *for this codebase* rather than a lapse.\n- Schema SQL and OpenAPI additions are placed consistently across all four dialects and in the right schema sections.\n\nNon-blocking nit (not requesting change): `java.time.Period.between(...)` is written fully-qualified while every other type in the file (including `java.time.LocalDate`) uses a top-level import \u2014 a tiny inconsistency a maintainer would likely tidy with an `import java.time.Period;`, but it doesn't affect the structure.",
